\pard\keep\keepn\widctlpar\s1\sb360\sa80\sl252\slmult1\cf1\kerning2\i\f0\fs40 The Great Gatsby\i0 : The Making of the Great Gatsby\par

\pard\keep\keepn\widctlpar\s2\sb160\sa80\sl252\slmult1\fs32 Prof Michael Nowlin\par

\pard\widctlpar\cf0\f1\fs24 University of Victoria\par
\par
Although F. Scott Fitzgerald was a remarkably successful young writer when he began thinking about his third novel, nobody could have imagined that he would write \ldblquote the best American novel ever written,\rdblquote  and those are his words to his editor at Scribners, Maxwell Perkins. He was still writing it and revise. He said that when he was writing it and revising late in the summer of 1924. And he knew better than anyone else how artistically superior it was to his earlier novels: \i This Side of Paradise\i0  (1920) and \i The Beautiful and Damned\i0  (1922).\par
\par
The loosely autobiographical \i This Side of Paradise\i0  had made a celebrity of Fitzgerald and his rather flamboyant, young wife, Zelda Sayre, of course, he married her just after \i the site of Paradis\i0 e was published. \i The Beautiful and Damned\i0  consolidated his reputation as a sort of chronicler of an emergent rebellious post-war youth culture and there was a lighter side to this constantly on display because he published popular short stories about this group in the \i Saturday Evening Post\i0 .\par
\par
The earlier novels are marked though by this era of pseudo sophistication, which probably owes a lot to the models he was drawing from. He was still too close to and these were British models in the case of the first novel, American naturalist and decadent novels in the case of the second. Both novels are quite, stylistically flamboyant, and he got a bit of a reputation for this, and also, formally, quite messy.\par
\par
So Fitzgerald, I think, was his own best critic, and soon after his second novel was published, he announced to Perkins his determination to take his writing to a whole other level. And this is what he wrote in 1922:\par
\par
\ldblquote I want to write something new \endash  something extraordinary and beautiful and simple and intricately patterned.\rdblquote\par

\pard\widctlpar\qr F. Scott Fitzgerald, letter to his editor Maxwell Perkins, July, 1922\par

\pard\widctlpar\par
Almost two years later in the spring of 1924, when he was about to write the novel in earnest, he reiterated to Perkins that it:\par
\par
\ldblquote will be a consciously artist achievement and must depend on that as the first books did not.\par
\par

\pard\widctlpar\qr F. Scott Fitzgerald, letter to his editor Maxwell Perkins, April, 1924\par

\pard\widctlpar\par
Now Joseph Conrad's fiction was a touchstone in that regard and he'd probably been learning to better appreciate this formal economy, and of Henry James's novels, for example. James was associated with a formal rigor, particularly in his use of point of view. Certainly \i The Great Gatsby\i0  is a tighter novel than his earlier ones. It says a lot more in fewer words. And it is indeed intricately patterned: held together by a coherent set of images, symbols, what we might call leitmotifs (a term taken over from music), that he might have been learning to use, through reading the Modernist writers that he was. He was discovering TS Eliot, James Joyce, Gertrude Stein, and I think the American writer who's often not considered a Modernist is probably the biggest influence on Fitzgerald is Willa Cather.\par
\par
So to his friend and former mentor H.L. Mencken, who failed to appreciate how good \i The Great Gatsby\i0  was, he declared that he wrote it \ldblquote in protest against his two earlier formless novels.\rdblquote  And that was by showcasing his command of novelistic form and his capacity for artistic detachments or aesthetic detachment. He was really seeking to elevate himself, to the terrain of the great contemporary novelists worldwide. I think the formal mastery that he showed in \i Gatsby\i0  enabled him to engage emotionally charged materials more effectively than he had in the earlier novels. \par
\par
And what is this emotional material? His rejection by this wealthy debutante, Ginevra King, during his, his college days at Princeton because she was a very, very wealthy woman from Lake Forest, Illinois.His long standing sense of having been a poor boy in a rich boy school when he was at Princeton, and even at his prep school earlier than that at Newman, his initial rejection by Zelda before \i This Side of Paradise\i0  got accepted and, most immediate: Zelda. It's almost certain Zelda had an affair in the summer of 1924 when he was writing \i Gatsby\i0  with a French, pilot named, Edouard Jozan. Now this extremely qualitative difference between \i Gatsby\i0  and \i The Beautiful and Damned\i0  owes a great deal to Fitzgerald's short story writing, which he sometimes disparaged for being too easy and of course he wrote short these short stories for money. But it's long been established by Fitzgerald scholars most notably, Matthew Bruccoli and James West III and Bryant Mangum, that the story writing functioned as a workshop of sorts through which Fitzgerald could work through themes and also enhance the quality of his prose.\par
\par
Following Bruccoli, most scholars now recognize what they call a \i Gatsby\i0  set of cluster stories, from 1922, up to the time he wrote \i Gatsby\i0 . The first of these is \lquote Winter Dreams,\rquote  which anticipates \i Gatsby\i0  and being so emphatically about romantic disillusionment. Another important story, from 1923, is called \lquote Absolution,\rquote  which concerns a young Catholic boy struggling, against the against the strictures of a pious mid-Western father \endash  immigrant father as well. And Fitzgerald had let Perkins know when he first was thinking about \i Gatsby\i0  that it was going to have a Catholic element, and it's widely assumed at one point he was thinking of \rquote Absolution\rquote  as a prelude to the main \i Gatsby\i0  story. He clearly dropped that idea. But again, I think \lquote Absolution\rquote  does suggest something about the immigrant background he had in mind for \i Gatsby\i0 . Other stories would include \lquote Dice, Brassknuckles & Guitar,\rquote  from 1923 and \lquote The Sensible Thing\rquote  from 1924 and these also explore certain themes that are in \i Gatsby\i0 .\par
\par
\i Gatsby\i0  was written mainly in France, where the Fitzgeralds sailed in 1924 for what would turn out to be about three years sojourn. He clearly needed to leave behind this busy boozy social world of New York City and Long Island which is where \i Gatsby\i0  takes place. So he needed to get distance from this world that would really be the background, crucial backdrop to his story (West Egg and East Egg, are fictionalized renditions of, Great Neck and Manhasset Neck on Long Island. Various real-life models have been suggested for Gatsby, the most plausible being a fellow named Max von Gerlach. But in essence, I think Gatsby seems largely derived from Fitzgerald's imagination and based on aspects of himself.\par
\par
In Southern France, he could focus on the novel with fewer disruptions, and of course it was much cheaper to live there. He was leaving behind him, curiously, an attempt to write a Broadway hit, a play called \i The Vegetable\i0 , which absolutely flopped at the end of 1923 when they tried it out at Atlantic City. So that's always been a bit of a curiosity for Fitzgerald scholars and readers: that he could leave behind something so forgettable, and then go on to write \i Gatsby\i0 , but clearly drama wasn't his fort\'e9.\par
\par
Once Fitzgerald was writing \i Gatsby\i0  throughout the summer and into the autumn of 1924, he progressed rather smoothly. By November, he sent Perkins a typescript, and that typescript's been subsequently lost but it's that typescript that Perkins read and commented on that would have quite an effect on the final form of \i Gatsby\i0 . Now, oddly enough Perkins, sent that typescript to be made into galley proofs, and galley proofs are usually the last form of a book that the author gets to see and make some corrections on before it goes to final press. Usually, you're not allowed to make very significant corrections. However, in the case of \i Gatsby\i0 , the changes were quite dramatic, and those changes came about, of course, because of Fitzgerald taking seriously some of Maxwell Perkins's criticisms about the draft he'd read.\par
\par
That early version is now available. It's published under the name \i Trimalchio\i0 , which was one of the titles that Fitzgerald had in mind, and published in Cambridge University Press\rquote s edition of, Fitzgerald's \i Complete Works\i0 . Perkins convinced Fitzgerald that there were two basic flaws with the book that you can read as \i Trimalchio\i0 .\par
\par
The first was that Fitzgerald had not adequately developed Gatsby's character. Perkins wanted a clear picture of him \endash  maybe a few more revealing traits, characteristics, and a clearer sense of how he made his money and, secondly, Fitzgerald actually had Gatsby tell all of his backstory in the first person, in chapter 8, and Perkins thought that undermined Fitzgerald's use of the more objective narrator, Nick Carraway. \par
\par
So, while traveling in Italy in 1925, Fitzgerald reworked the novel from the galley proofs. He extensively rewrote certain passages, which again could change the characterization, but I think, most radically, he broke up that backstory and he moved parts of it into earlier sections of the novel. Again, we learn crucial things at the beginning of chapter 6, for example, and that gets filtered through Nick Carraway's narration and is not told in the first person by Gatsby. So, the extent that, \i Gatsby\i0  is so often regarded as a nearly perfect novel it owes some of that perfection to Max Perkins's initial suggestions.\par
\par
Fitzgerald was still juggling titles in the spring of 1925. He had already been through \i Trimalchio\i0  and \i Trimalchio\i0  \i on West Egg\i0  and Trimalchio is the name of a former slave who gives these lavish banquets in Petronius' satire, \i Satyricon\i0 , and a new translation of that had become available in 1922. Three weeks before the book was published, Fitzgerald suddenly suggested a new title: \i Under the Red, White, and Blue\i0 . For better, for worse, it was too late. So it appeared in 1925 as \i The Great Gatsby\i0 .\par
\par
With Francis Cugat\rquote s now famous, almost iconic, cover of a woman's eyes over an amusement park, at night. Fitzgerald had seen the original gouache painting of that in the Scribner's office before he left for France and he actually wrote that image into the novel.\par

